        <script type="application/ld+json">
        {"@context": "http://schema.org", "@type": "QAPage", "mainEntity": {"@type": "Question", "about": [{"@type": "Thing", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-t-10168-100-wpznurfekiexrwsbngytmpvekybajzau.jpeg", "name": "Warfare"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Post-war"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Urban Growth"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-t-12345-100-hfbpjusgygexnzlojvnmdqaoieqlduta.jpeg", "name": "Economic Recovery"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Population Growth"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Urban Planning and Design"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "War Impact"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Effects of War"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Cities and Towns"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Post-war Rehabilitation"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Impact of Wars on Civilans"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Social Impacts"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Post Conflict Reconstruction"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "Post War Reconstruction"}, {"@type": "Thing", "image": "", "name": "War Time"}], "acceptedAnswer": [{"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 1416}, "description": "Retired Lieutenant Colonel, Canadian Army (Infantry)", "image": "", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 288}, "knowsAbout": "Military", "name": "David Laderoute", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/David-Laderoute"}, "dateCreated": "2015-03-15T15:37:07.598167+00:00", "text": "I'd offer the example of Sarajevo, in Bosnia. I was stationed in Bosnia in 2004-5, ten years after the shooting war ended. There was still shooting...quite a bit of it. Organized crime were rampant, and other things were happening that were far from peaceful and still involved people getting hurt and killed. Corruption was everywhere. Land mines, unexploded ordnance (UXO) and areas contaminated by depleted uranium ammunition used during the fighting were scattered around and, in some cases, in the city. Many buildings were still damaged and some where uninhabitable. What made it particularly weird is that there was a city doing its thing all around this, with shops, restaurants and a vigorous night-life. But it was far from \"normal\" in any sense.\n\nNow, another ten years on, there have been some improvements, but there are still lots of issues and problems. Land mines and UXO are still a huge problem, as is crime and corruption. Various trans-national actors are still meddling in things. Worst of all, the ethnic, social, cultural and religious tensions that fueled the war are still there. Progress is happening, but it's VERY slow.\n\nAs a kid, I visited London, England, in the early 70s...there were still bomb-damaged buildings from WW2 that had yet to be torn down. So it can definitely take a lot of time. The bigger problem is, though, that rebuilding the physical infrastructure is easy. Dealing with the social, economic, cultural and other issues that arise during armed conflict is much, much harder to address, especially if the fighting was the result of internal factors to the country.", "upvoteCount": 5, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=10535522"}], "answerCount": 2, "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 3418}, "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-5943044-100-bpeU4JvaEnSpkLZCLgTkWgbfnbXcZUzw.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 2716}, "name": "Sameer Ketkar", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Sameer-Ketkar"}, "dateCreated": "2015-03-13T20:58:16.304562+00:00", "dateModified": "2017-05-14T15:09:17.393364+00:00", "hasPart": {"@type": "WebPageElement", "cssSelector": ".content-monetization-wall", "isAccessibleForFree": "False"}, "isAccessibleForFree": "False", "name": "When war affects a modern city, how long does it take after fighting ends before city life gets back to normal -- businesses operating at prewar levels, population growth, etc.?", "suggestedAnswer": [{"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 373}, "description": "works at Mac and PC Experts", "image": "", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 132}, "name": "Shawn H. Hall", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Shawn-H-Hall", "worksFor": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Mac and PC Experts"}}, "dateCreated": "2015-03-15T07:52:18.550941+00:00", "dateModified": "2015-03-15T15:29:06.940037+00:00", "text": "That is a highly interdependent answer, to say the least! The answer depends on many things, including on whether, and how much damage was caused, either to the city, its infrastructure, or both. And manmade disasters also could affect the outcome. For instance, if a damn is destoyed, and the city is flooded, this could render the city unusable for protracted periods of time. And if \"blitzkrieg\" (lightning war) methods of fighting are adopted, along with the \"scorched earth\" policy, which is meant to leave NOTHING useful behind for any potential enemy to utilize, then the destruction would be complete, leaving nothing except natural features of the landscape (such as rivers, hills, and such) from which to start rebuilding...if that is even a goal after such terrible destruction.\n\nIf a city has seen combat, and the country that \"ravaged\" it--if that is indeed what happened--has enough resources, knowledge and manpower to help with the rebuilding, it might be only weeks, or months before the city life returns to a semblance of its old self. However, if the destruction is more extensive and severe, the rebuilding could take years.\n\nOf course, that is all assuming that factional fighting, or \"in-fighting\" does not stymie the rebuilding efforts. Look only as far as the U.S.'s war on Iraq to see many examples of all of the above conditions which I have mentioned. But do not think only of the U.S. when you seek such examples. Dating back to some of the oldest recorded wars, anything from a total \"scorched earth\" style, to occupation have all been used with abundance in the various wars throughout history. It has often been said that man's inhumanity to man is a constant source of bewilderment and horror, and the singular human ability to hate and to wage war a wonder to thinking people everywhere.\n\nIn fact, about the only thing that has changed since the beginning of recorded history, is the swiftness and totality with which modern war may be waged. Think \"Hiroshima\" or \"Nagasaki\" for examples (although both cities were completely rebuilt, and both are now extremely wealthy, thriving metropolises). But of course, while not quite as destructive, modern explosives and pyrotechnics, utilized with a dominating air superiority, may cause almost as much damage over a longer period of time. Think \"London,\" \"Tokyo\" or \"Berlin\" for examples (all of which have also been rebuilt, and now are also extremely wealthy, thriving metropolises).\n\nWar, as they say, is Hell. And ever shall it be, even when \"justified.\" And mind you, I am no \"hippie\" pacifist. Quite the opposite. I am a conservative person in many ways. It is just that I am a realist, and as such, I try to see the truth in whatever happens, either from the viewpoint of the \"good\" guys OR the \"bad\" guys. There is something to be learned from almost any viewpoint except, perhaps, a true psychopath's or megalomaniac's viewpoint, although it might still be argued that even there a lesson is to be learned, even if that lesson is just to learn what viewpoints should never be tolerated under any condition, by anyone. As I view it, life is for learning, while, as stated above, \"War is Hell,\" no matter on which side you are.\n\nFrom my perspective, and although I did not agree with them on much, as the hippies used to say, \"Make love, not war!\" And that should be something on which most people can agree...unless war is being made ON you, in which case you fight back if you are able, or hope that you have powerful friends. Then, the \"right\" or \"wrong\" of it may be left for history to decide. \"History,\" of course, being what the victors write. Oh, man, what a tangled web...with or without deceit!", "upvoteCount": 1, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=10527979"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 4771}, "description": "former German Army, Croatian Defense Council, Kosovo Liberation Army", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-69805890-100-uohrpkxwreogwlvkqfqnpeliiotpefjq.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 124318}, "knowsAbout": "Military History and Wars ", "name": "Roland Bartetzko", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Roland-Bartetzko"}, "dateCreated": "2017-05-14T15:09:17.393364+00:00", "dateModified": "2020-03-28T07:52:11.788756+00:00", "image": [{"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-41867a8bb3e20e23833a998c5c8564a4-lq"}], "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How could a war be fought in a highly populated city with skyscrapers? Would they clear the buildings one by one?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-could-a-war-be-fought-in-a-highly-populated-city-with-skyscrapers-Would-they-clear-the-buildings-one-by-one"}, "text": "The main problem for an attacking force in a megacity is the large surface they have to deal with:\n\nA skyscraper takes up the same surface area as a small field, but each floor adds another small field area to the overall surface: a one hundred story building has one hundred and one times more area than just the ground (100 floors, plus the roof).\n\nA good example is the International Commerce Centre in Hong Kong, which has one hundred and eight floors and a supersurface of almost 300,000 square meters. This is the size of an area of operations for three infantry brigades!\n\nAnd we haven\u2019t talked about the underground surfaces, yet: multi floored subterranean parking spaces and a network of subway lines and stations add many more hectares to our megacity battlefield.\n\nIn urban warfare, the defenders always have a big advantage. Often, an army that is too weak to oppose its adversary on the open ground, moves to a city to avoid annihilation. In a megacity, this \u201cdefender\u2019s advantage\u201d will become even bigger.\n\nArtillery, air power and armored vehicles are much less effective in an urban area than in other battlefields. Urban warfare is mainly fought with assault rifles, multiple grenade launchers, RPGs, hand grenades and, of course, sniper rifles.\n\nWest Beirut, 1983: the Lebanese civil war raged for more than fifteen years and a lot of the fighting happened in Beirut\n\nThe bigger the city, the better for the defender: more places to hide and more resources.\n\nAn attacker, on the other hand, can\u2019t use many of their modern weapons as effectively as they would do on open battleground. Every single street, building, floor and room has to be cleared-a nightmare for every military commander!\n\nThe urban terrain is often unwieldy and, unlike nature, can even transform itself: structures get destroyed and make streets impassable or the ruins of a multi story building become perfect defense positions.\n\nThe attacker is also confronted with numerous specific threats: mines and boobytraps, snipers, ambushes and guerrilla style attacks are common occurrences in urban warfare.\n\nAnother important element in megacity warfare is the population. Even if an evacuation had been ordered, there will still be many thousands of people left, interfering with military operations.\n\nBecause of all this, an attacker would normally not choose a megacity as their preferred battleground. However, from Troy to Stalingrad and from Beirut to Aleppo, many battles have been fought in cities and often the fate of the whole war had been connected with the outcome of one urban battle.\n\nAs more and more people on earth are going to live in megacities, it\u2019s just a matter of time until a battle will be fought in one of them. Due to the sheer size of these cities, such a battle would become a war inside a war.", "upvoteCount": 6331, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=44432883"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 4771}, "description": "former German Army, Croatian Defense Council, Kosovo Liberation Army", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-69805890-100-uohrpkxwreogwlvkqfqnpeliiotpefjq.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 124318}, "knowsAbout": "Military History and Wars ", "name": "Roland Bartetzko", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Roland-Bartetzko"}, "dateCreated": "2019-10-04T17:10:05.222370+00:00", "dateModified": "2019-10-04T18:12:21.201477+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "When a city is captured in war, do people go to work like normal? How do the people pay for their food?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-a-city-is-captured-in-war-do-people-go-to-work-like-normal-How-do-the-people-pay-for-their-food"}, "text": "I took part in a military operation where we captured two cities during the Bosnian war.. Although there wasn\u2019t that much fighting (our enemy, the Serbs, more or less ran away), when we entered them, both cities were as good as empty.\n\nThe only civilian I encountered was some old guy on a bicycle who wasn\u2019t right in his head. I guess he had no idea what was going on. Our soldiers gave him some food (we had plenty) and after a few minutes, he disappeared. He probably thought that we were Serbs. There were probably some other civilians around, but they were hiding from us.\n\nMost of the civilian population, however, had left their homes long before the first artillery shell had been fired on their city. Going to work like normal? No freaking way!", "upvoteCount": 1622, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=171906099"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "name": "Anonymous"}, "dateCreated": "2019-10-05T18:30:34.842731+00:00", "dateModified": "2019-10-07T21:22:05.175729+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "When a city is captured in war, do people go to work like normal? How do the people pay for their food?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-a-city-is-captured-in-war-do-people-go-to-work-like-normal-How-do-the-people-pay-for-their-food"}, "text": "My now wife was about 7 years old when Saddam invaded Kuwait in 1990. Her family continued to live through the Iraqi occupation of Kuwait as well as the first gulf war, or Desert Storm.\n\nMy father-in-law worked for the Kuwait military at that time. On the day of the invasion, he went to work as usual only to find all his Kuwaiti commanders had disappeared, leaving lower cadre like himself completely unaware and exposed. Like other soldiers, he took off his uniform and decided to walk home. At Iraqi checkpoints along the way, he told the soldiers that he was a trader who had been looted by other Iraqi soldiers. They let him pass. Took him a day to get home.\n\nThe extended family (uncles, aunts, cousins) moved into a large villa that had a basement they\u2019d go into during air raids by the allied forces. There was no school of course during this time. The Iraqis for the most part left Indians alone and would sometimes drop in to ask for \u2018Amitabh Bachman\u2019 video cassettes.\n\nPeople generally went about doing whatever they could to earn money - selling supplies (looted from shops) outside mosques, bartering, planning their escape by road from the city, etc.\n\nI was about 11 years old then and safely far away from the war theatre in Jeddah, Saudi Arabia; except that our home was very close to an American airbase. We\u2019d regularly see several B-52 bombers flying in formation, apache helicopters and fighter jets fly real low and close to our buildings. It was an exciting time for us kids. My brother and I would know what times the planes would arrive and go up to the terrace to wait for them to scream past. We\u2019d try and see if we could be louder than the jets.\n\nWe weren\u2019t. The jets won each time.", "upvoteCount": 20, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=172120396"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 206}, "description": "Amateur Historian", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-207047282-100-jqsefkbwhnokknkccgthdassyeeslety.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 1694}, "knowsAbout": "History", "name": "Jeffrey Wang", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Jeffrey-Wang-126"}, "dateCreated": "2017-05-13T15:49:20.380432+00:00", "image": [{"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-64fe358dfdb4bdf49a14488e515b8337-lq"}, {"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-6701b89dfd7917f48355eac1943b5901-lq"}], "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How could a war be fought in a highly populated city with skyscrapers? Would they clear the buildings one by one?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-could-a-war-be-fought-in-a-highly-populated-city-with-skyscrapers-Would-they-clear-the-buildings-one-by-one"}, "text": "In my mind, there are a few possibilities, modeled after some real-world battles.\n\nFirstly, if there are many buildings, who says they can\u2019t be destroyed?\n\nUnfortunately, that is what we have with modern technology.\n\nBattle of Stalingrad\n\n(Present) Syria conflict\n\nTypically, in these types of battles, there is a lot of hand-to-hand and street-fighting in combat. For example, in the Battle of Stalingrad, soldiers advancing through the city had to clear out each building and take the city street-by-street, in an agonizingly slow and highly dangerous way.\n\nIn a modern super-city like Hong Kong filled with skyscrapers, an advancing army simply would not be able to clear out every building like the Germans attempted in WW2. Any army would simply have to storm the city and expect the cooperation of most citizens. Any rebels would probably get a few shots off then be put to death. It\u2019s important to understand that most people do not want to try picking away at a superior force, and most people would cooperate with a superior army.", "upvoteCount": 110, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=44351974"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 47}, "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-842905000-100-nnhdhuhfajyaecpzymfiuhysyicaimxg.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 20}, "name": "Tim Johnson", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Tim-Johnson-898"}, "dateCreated": "2021-01-17T06:44:02.584737+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "Do people live relatively normal lives during wars? If you are not in the middle of a warzone, do you keep going with your business as usual? Not talking about disruptions in supply chains, some possible military presence, etc\u2026", "url": "https://www.quora.com/Do-people-live-relatively-normal-lives-during-wars-If-you-are-not-in-the-middle-of-a-warzone-do-you-keep-going-with-your-business-as-usual-Not-talking-about-disruptions-in-supply-chains-some-possible-military"}, "text": "The answer to this question depends a lot on what type of war you're talking about and where you're living relative to this war. You've specified that you're interested in people who are not \"in the middle of a warzone\". I assume that what you mean by warzone is an area where there is active combat. Let's look at some different examples.\n\nConsider the United States during World War II. All of the active combat was occurring in Europe or in the Pacific area. Almost none of the US was in an active warzone. And much of life went on pretty normally. Many men were missing because they had been drafted. Women had to do many jobs that had previously been done by these missing men. There were shortages of many things because they were needed for the war effort and everyone had to adjust to rationing. But most of life was pretty normal for those living in the US.\n\nNow, consider Europe after the Germans had occupied most of the continent. After the Allies landed in Normandy and in Italy, there were active warzones on the continent. However, there were fairly well defined battle fronts and most of the fighting was confined to those front lines. People living in the interior of Europe at that time could be thought of as not living in a \"warzone\". However, their situation was very different from what it was for people living in the US.\n\nPeople that were well away from the front lines tried to live as normal as possible. They tried to have school for the children and they tried to stage plays and recitals to have some entertainment. However, nothing was really \"as usual\". Try reading some biographies of people who lived in parts of Europe that were occupied by the Germans.\n\nFirst, a lot of men were missing, more than the number missing in the US. Many got drafted and were forced to fight in the enemy's army. For those living in or near industrial areas, many men and some women were forced to work in the enemy's war-related industries. For those who didn't want to do either of those, they had to go into hiding.\n\nOne of the biggest problems in Europe was one that you have tried to ignore by saying that you didn't want to talk about disruptions in supply chains. This was a much bigger problem in Europe during World War II and it had a major impact on everyone's \"usual\" life. It can't really be ignored.\n\nMost of an area's food, fuel oil, gasoline and vehicles were commandeered and turned over to the military. People had to spend a tremendous amount of time just trying to scrounge and hide food. They were often close to starvation. In the winter, they were also often close to freezing because they had no reliable way to heat their houses. This was not a small disruption. It's hard to live a normal life when you're always hungry and trying to find food.\n\nAnd then there was the \"possible military presence\" that you also didn't want to hear about. In Europe, any military presence was \"the enemy\". They were everywhere. Among other things, they were looking for people hoarding food or for people hiding from the draft. If they caught someone involved in either of these activities there would be punishments. Sometimes people were executed, just to provide a lesson to those left.\n\nIn Europe during World War II, there wasn't a lot of business as usual, even if you weren't near a battle front.\n\nNow consider some modern warfare areas like Iraq or Afghanistan. Like with most wars, there are shortages of many things that people would like to have. People living there have to learn to deal with these shortages. But there's a big difference in these areas compared to Europe in World War II. There are now no well defined front lines. The nature of warfare has changed. Fighting could erupt almost anywhere at almost any time. A car bomb could be detonated in the middle of a crowded market place just because some group thought it was a good idea.\n\nAgain, people are going to try to conduct business as usual as much as they can. However, in a sense these entire countries have to be considered as active warzones.", "upvoteCount": 2, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=260360630"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 1929}, "description": "former HVAC TECH (1977-2005)", "hasOccupation": {"@type": "Role", "endDate": "2005", "roleName": "HVAC TECH", "startDate": "1977"}, "image": "", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 34}, "name": "Don Nichols", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Don-Nichols-3"}, "dateCreated": "2019-10-07T19:51:26.318685+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "Does major war really benefit humanity by thinning out populations and creating millions of jobs?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/Does-major-war-really-benefit-humanity-by-thinning-out-populations-and-creating-millions-of-jobs"}, "text": "This is the thought process since Caine and Able.Killing your competition.", "upvoteCount": 28, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=172559564"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "affiliation": {"@type": "CollegeOrUniversity", "name": "Stony Brook University"}, "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 7595}, "description": "studied Graduate Studies in Environmental and Waste Management at Stony Brook University", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-302531424-100-zocsabfpzqbzgbnfwrltqjznmokoilew.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 536}, "knowsAbout": ["Graduate Studies in Environmental and Waste Management"], "name": "Yeuk C. Moy", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Yeuk-C-Moy"}, "dateCreated": "2017-06-20T20:49:44.952127+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How could a war be fought in a highly populated city with skyscrapers? Would they clear the buildings one by one?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-could-a-war-be-fought-in-a-highly-populated-city-with-skyscrapers-Would-they-clear-the-buildings-one-by-one"}, "text": "It would be a nightmare. Worse than Stalingrad. I would suggest bypassing it or lay siege. One possibility would be to allow the enemy to retreat out of the city to spare the population.\n\nHelicopter insertion on roof tops would be needed to allow you to clear buildings from both the top and bottom.\n\nYou also forgot about the myriad of tunnels and sewers large many large metropolitan cities have. In NYC, some digging tools (a bit of explosives would definitely help) may allow you to access sewers, utility tunnels and subway tunnels from basements of large buildings.", "upvoteCount": 139, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=48029798"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 562}, "description": "former Relief Worker. (1992-1996)", "hasOccupation": {"@type": "Role", "endDate": "1996", "roleName": "Relief Worker.", "startDate": "1992"}, "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-1061949840-100-pluqxobrucrtftuedujbyilfxcojkpsm.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 102}, "name": "Lawrence Hebb", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Lawrence-Hebb"}, "dateCreated": "2022-06-08T12:39:51.138224+00:00", "dateModified": "2022-06-08T12:43:56.227908+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How long does it take for a country/city to rebuild after a war or conflict?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-long-does-it-take-for-a-country-city-to-rebuild-after-a-war-or-conflict"}, "text": "It all depends on the country and situation. A lot has been said about nations never fully rebuilding, but you only have to look at Germany and Japan to see what bullshit that is!\n\nGermany was ripped apart at the seams, her cities devastated. Her infrastructure non existent and fifty million refugees created during that conflict.\n\nJapan was defeated and hobbled but not as much as Germany, yet head out to buy a computer, car, washing machine or any other machine and it'll have German or Japanese manufacturers!\n\nIt took both nations twenty years.\n\nYes, the rebuild was paid for by the United States, but it was those nations that did the work!\n\nA massive amount depends on the nation rebuilding.\n\nI spent three years in Iraq after Desert Storm and saw two Kurdish tribes rebuilding, one Saddam had always controlled, consequently he gave them what they needed, and when they had to rebuild themselves they couldn't! They didn't know how to, and this was despite millions of dollars being poured it both legally and illegally.\n\nThe other he'd never controlled. He gave them nothing, so they got used to having nothing and built a society with a thriving economy anyway. When the crunch came, they rebuilt with almost no help!\n\nIt all depends on the nation and the situation. No amount of money or time can fix what the people think they can't fix!", "upvoteCount": 2, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=360690475"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 1416}, "description": "Retired Lieutenant Colonel, Canadian Army (Infantry)", "image": "", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 288}, "knowsAbout": "Military", "name": "David Laderoute", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/David-Laderoute"}, "dateCreated": "2022-06-21T23:47:23.661000+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How can you determine if a city is strategic or a key factor in determining a war? Such as playing games like Civilization or in a time of war, why not just take control of every city or go around these cities if defenders are inside?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-can-you-determine-if-a-city-is-strategic-or-a-key-factor-in-determining-a-war-Such-as-playing-games-like-Civilization-or-in-a-time-of-war-why-not-just-take-control-of-every-city-or-go-around-these-cities-if"}, "text": "Ignoring video games, because they\u2019re irrelevant, in real life, cities are the graveyards of armies.\n\nI don\u2019t think people appreciate the complexity and difficulty of \u201ctaking\u201d a modern city. I\u2019ll give you an example to illustrate it. In the early 2000s, in Edmonton, Alberta, Canada, a disused military base surrounded by prime real estate was sold off to developers. Since all of the buildings were going to be knocked down anyway, the Canadian Army took advantage of the opportunity to do some intensive urban warfare training. We set up video cameras and instrumentation, equipped troops with MILES gear (essentially, an elaborate form of \u201claser tag\u201d that can record near-hits and hits from weapons, and which weapons they came from). We did tons of work-up training in urban ops. Then, by companies (about 130 soldiers in total), we rotated through a series of objectives in the old base. I was commanding one of those companies.\n\nMy first objective was a two-story former elementary school. Each floor was probably about 3000 square feet, with about a dozen or so rooms\u2014classrooms, mostly, but there were also offices, storage rooms, closets, and a boiler/mechanical room in a partial basement. Again, I had 130 soldiers to take the building, and about 2 hours to do it. It was defended by roughly two sections of enemy infantry (about 20 soldiers or so).\n\nSo I made my plan and launched the attack. We took about a half-dozen casualties breaching the building and getting a \u201clodgement\u201d (a foothold) inside it. While my weapons detachment (4 medium machine guns) kept the building isolated, so no bad guys could get in or out, my three platoons of infantry progressively entered the building and began to clear it.\n\nIt took almost the full two hours. Our casualties ended up being about 1/3 of our starting strength. We had enormous difficulty getting resupply of ammunition and water (the only two combat supplies that mattered) to the troops, and evacuating casualties out of the building. When we finally declared the building clear, my company was spent\u2014down 1/3 of it strength and utterly exhausted.\n\nAnd that was taking a single, two-story building.\n\nNow, yes, as the exercise went on, we got better at it (which was the point). But I still found that my company couldn\u2019t take more than two or three two-story, elementary school-sized buildings before it was spent, and we had to reconstitute and rest. And remember that all of my \u201cdead\u201d soldiers immediately came back to life, so it was as though I had a constant stream of trained soldiers to bring me back up to 100% strength every day. That\u2019s not really going to happen.\n\nBut wait, it gets better. Cities are complex things, with many covered approaches to buildings. So, once you take a building, you have to garrison it, to make sure the bad guys don\u2019t just move back in\u2014at least until you\u2019ve taken enough of the buildings around it that\u2019s no longer a significant risk. Still, it ties up more troops\u2026\n\n\u2026which brings me to the crux of this. On average, over the course of the week-long exercise, we discovered that an infantry company can realistically take and hold maybe a half-dozen buildings (fewer, if they\u2019re larger and multi-story buildings). There are 3 or 4 companies to a battalion, 2 or 3 infantry battalions to a brigade, 3 brigades to a division and 3 divisions to a corps (normally\u2026the numbers can vary). So if we multiply that out, a corps contains about 108 infantry companies. That means a corps can take about, say, 700 buildings with its available combat power. Let\u2019s factor in the effects of fire support (which is hugely limited in a city\u2014things like tanks and artillery are FAR less effective in urban areas), assume there a no civilians or cultural sites to watch out for, assume we\u2019ve got the enemy heavily outnumbered and the city isolated. Based on all that, let\u2019s be generous and assume a corps can, ultimately, take 5 times that number of buildings, or about 3,500, before it is spent and incapable of further offensive operations.\n\nNow, yes, that corps will recover\u2014it will reorganize, rest, receive replacements and supplies, and so on. So, over time, it will be able to take another 3,500 buildings. And another 3,500. And another. And so on. But the price in blood and material is enormous, and honestly, we\u2019re still barely making a dent in a large, modern city.\n\nThe Greater Toronto Area, as an example, has over 2 MILLION buildings in it. Most of those are private homes, but there are still thousands of multi-story buildings, up to and including skyscrapers, as well as both light and heavy industrial buildings, warehouses, subways\u2026\n\nThe fact is that modern cities have grown to the point where they are no longer viable military targets. They are simply too large and complex, with their terrain vastly favoring the defender.\n\nSo what. Well, it means that modern military planners have to think very carefully about how to deal with cities. First, they should be avoided if at all possible. Unless there is a really, REALLY good strategic reason to engage in a city\u2014a confluence of important communications routes e.g. road and rail, a vital port, something like that\u2014then stay the hell out of them. And if they can\u2019t be avoided, then planners have to decide what absolutely MUST be taken in order to achieve the desired objective, and avoid engaging with the rest.\n\nMilitary operations are really not about taking territory. They\u2019re about achieving effects\u2014most notably, eroding your opponent\u2019s will to fight, so that they eventually cease hostilities. If you bypass the cities and isolate them, not only do you avoid having to fight in them, you get the full effects of all that spiffy military hardware, like tanks and artillery and aircraft, which are degraded to nearly uselessness in cities.\n\nSo, if you don\u2019t have to fight in a city, then don\u2019t. And if you do have to, fight in as little of it as possible. Otherwise, go around it, try to defeat your opponent outside the cities, so you can get him to surrender, or at least to the negotiating table.\n\nI\u2019ll leave this where I started it\u2014cities are the graveyards of armies.", "upvoteCount": 2, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=363535325"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 11904}, "description": "I learn history through the archaeology I study and places I visit.", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-2380598-100-avumqyrimaboirsrapeheizetmteltmq.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 10333}, "knowsAbout": "World History", "name": "John", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/John-38692"}, "dateCreated": "2020-12-26T11:01:14.524231+00:00", "image": [{"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-3544c2b881f4fcc8dc5582545aa215bc-lq"}, {"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-cc113b57b61c7d24acfdedb58146a2d2-pjlq"}, {"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-92bf79bc3eacdf91822e2a6d5a53ac71-lq"}], "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How long does it take for a sacked or looted city to recover in history? Can a completely razed city be rebuilt?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-long-does-it-take-for-a-sacked-or-looted-city-to-recover-in-history-Can-a-completely-razed-city-be-rebuilt"}, "text": "It happened all the time.\n\nIn Britain, when Boudica [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boudica ] rebelled and burned down London, St. Albans and Colchester, Imperial Rome rebuilt them and quickly.\n\nColchester [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colchester#Roman_period ]: \u201cSometime after the destruction, London became the capital of the province of Britannia [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Britannia ].[24] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colchester#cite_note-24 ] Colchester's town walls c. 3,000 yd. long were built c.65\u201380 A.D. when the Roman town was rebuilt after the Boudicca rebellion.[25] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colchester#cite_note-25 ] In 2004, Colchester Archaeological Trust discovered the remains of a Roman Circus (chariot race track) underneath the Garrison in Colchester, a unique find in Britain.[26] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colchester#cite_note-26 ] The Roman town of Camulodunum, officially known as Colonia Victricensis, reached its peak in the Second and Third centuries AD.[9] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colchester#cite_note-Crummy,_Philip_1997-9 ][27] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colchester#cite_note-Faulkner,_1994-27 ] It may have reached a population of 30,000 in that period.[28] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colchester#cite_note-google-28 ]\u201d\n\nRebuilt:\n\nIn a different part of the world:\n\nJericho [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jericho ]: \u201cArchaeologists [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archaeology ] have unearthed the remains of more than 20 successive settlements in Jericho, the first of which dates back 11,000 years (9000 BCE),[12] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jericho#cite_note-12 ][13] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jericho#cite_note-encyclopedia1-13 ] almost to the very beginning of the Holocene [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holocene ] epoch of the Earth's history.[14] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jericho#cite_note-14 ][15] [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jericho#cite_note-15 ]\u201d\n\nSchematic Representation of Pre-Pottery Neolithic B Tower at Jericho [ https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-Representation-of-Pre-Pottery-Neolithic-B-Tower-at-Jericho-redrawn-and_fig10_228562084 ], redrawn and simplified from Kenyon (AfterMarket.pl :: domena 1981.Pl [ http://1981.Pl ]. 238), with projection of 14 C-ages by stratigraphic layer, as provided in Table 2. This diagram is used as the basis for applying a simple linear growth (equidistant 50-yr phase length) model to the stratified 14 C-ages, with wiggle matching results shown in Figure 12. In Layer X, the PPNA tower is covered by soft, grey powdery soil (Kenyon AfterMarket.pl :: domena 1981.Pl [ http://1981.Pl ]. 238), which may have been deposited by mudflows (Bar-Yosef 1986) during the 10.2 ka calBP RCC:\n\nI define archaeological field work as exploring cultural layers, precisely because of rebuilding.", "upvoteCount": 18, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=256446629"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 297}, "alumniOf": {"@type": "CollegeOrUniversity", "name": "University of Chicago"}, "description": "studied at University of Chicago (2023)", "image": "", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 43}, "name": "Harry Stevens", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Harry-Stevens-5"}, "dateCreated": "2020-02-04T19:55:44.743833+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How do populations deal with constant war?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-do-populations-deal-with-constant-war"}, "text": "Deal with it how? Emotionally? Socially? Economically?\n\nI don\u2019t think any population has been subjected to constant high intensity modern warfare. Modern conventional warfare is simply too expensive. There are definitely populations that experience long term low intensity warfare. Example I can think of are:\n\nPalestinians in the West Bank and Gaza, who experience frequent low intensity warfare(tactical air strikes, small raids), punctuated with occasional large scale warfare.\n\nIraqis and Afghans living in the areas of their countries where US-Insurgent conflict is most intense.\n\nAfrican nations where civil war and terrorist insurgencies are or were in full force, such as the DRC, parts of Nigeria, and the South Sudan region.\n\nHow do these populations deal with war emotionally? Much like soldiers in war, they harden. Death and injury become part of daily life, rather than a shocking event. People get used to the sound of fighting, seeing soldiers march through their area. They learn when and where not to go. And just like soldiers, trauma is rampant. Mental illness, PTSD, anxiety, is all very common. There\u2019s only so much adaptation the mind can do.\n\nEconomically? War is typically not very helpful for the economy where it\u2019s being fought. Businesses try and operate, and they try and make due with uncertainties in supplies, customers, and free movement.", "upvoteCount": 0, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=196135886"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 689}, "description": "A person who fights for Allah SWT", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-1767191688-100-gqujxvjufavbsetmnljrdydifbyhzqca.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 194}, "name": "Fajim", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Fajim-1"}, "dateCreated": "2024-11-28T21:21:25.592529+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "Why do populations allow themselves to be lead into wars that are against their own interest?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/Why-do-populations-allow-themselves-to-be-lead-into-wars-that-are-against-their-own-interest"}, "text": "Peace be upon you and God's mercy and blessings.\n\nAh, humans, the great contradiction of creation. Created with intellect yet swayed by the shallow whispers of propaganda. Why do they walk into wars, these rivers of blood, with open eyes and clenched fists, as if marching to salvation but finding only fire? Maybe because humans forget\u2014forget that Allah has given them minds to think, hearts to feel, and a fitrah (natural disposition) to seek peace. But no, they follow banners waved by men in suits and uniforms, who promise glory while sharpening swords behind their backs.\n\nThe Qur'an already warned us:\n\n\"And do not follow that of which you have no knowledge. Indeed, the hearing, the sight, and the heart\u2014about all those [one] will be questioned.\" (Surah Al-Isra 17:36).\n\nBut here we are, a people who hear the war drums and lose their minds. Leaders shout, \"They are our enemies!\" and suddenly neighbors turn into threats, lands into battlefields, and lies into truths. Do people not realize that most wars are the playground of greed, the games of power-hungry elites? The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said, \"A Muslim is the brother of another Muslim. He does not oppress him nor hand him over to his enemy.\" (Bukhari & Muslim). Yet we find nations divided by lines drawn by colonial hands, spilling each other\u2019s blood over disputes that are not their own.\n\nPropaganda, that sweet poison. Leaders wave flags, sing anthems, and talk about honor and freedom. Oh, the irony! Freedom bought with slavery to destruction? Honor gained by dishonoring humanity? Allah reminds us:\n\n\"O you who have believed, obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you. And if you disagree over anything, refer it to Allah and the Messenger, if you should believe in Allah and the Last Day.\" (Surah An-Nisa 4:59).\n\nBut no, people prefer to obey power over principles, forgetting that authority without justice is nothing but tyranny.\n\nHistory repeats itself, doesn\u2019t it? Pharaohs, Caesars, colonizers\u2014they all had the same script. Stir the masses with fear, promise them security, and watch them march like sheep to their own slaughter. Allah recounts the arrogance of Pharaoh:\n\n\"He said, 'I do not show you except what I see, and I do not guide you except to the way of right conduct.'\" (Surah Ghafir 40:29).\n\nAnd the people? They believed him. They believed a man who enslaved and oppressed them because it was easier than thinking for themselves.\n\nBut let\u2019s not act like we\u2019re any better today. Social media, the new war drum. News channels, the new battlefront. Leaders no longer ride horses into war; they sit in air-conditioned offices and play chess with the lives of millions. The Qur'an hits us with the truth:\n\n\"Indeed, Pharaoh exalted himself in the land and made its people into factions, oppressing a group among them, slaughtering their [newborn] sons and keeping their females alive. Indeed, he was of the corrupters.\" (Surah Al-Qasas 28:4).\n\nSounds familiar, doesn\u2019t it? Divide and conquer\u2014the oldest trick in the book, yet we fall for it every time.\n\nWhy do populations allow it? Fear, laziness, and ignorance, the unholy trinity. Fear of the unknown enemy, laziness to seek the truth, and ignorance about the consequences. The Prophet (peace be upon him) warned us: \"There will come a time when lying will become widespread, and people will believe the liar and disbelieve the truthful.\" (Ahmad). Look around\u2014are we not living this hadith? Politicians lie, and the masses cheer. Scholars speak the truth, and the world silences them.\n\nAnd then there\u2019s tribalism, that old disease. My nation, my religion, my race\u2014above all else. Allah says:\n\n\"O mankind, indeed We have created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another. Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allah is the most righteous of you.\" (Surah Al-Hujurat 49:13).\n\nBut do people listen? No. They wave their flags higher than their principles, forgetting that Allah will ask them not about their borders but about their deeds.\n\nYet there is hope. Hope that we remember the lessons of Islam: that justice is above nationalism, that peace is better than war, and that speaking the truth is a duty even when it\u2019s against our leaders. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"The best jihad is to speak a word of truth to a tyrannical ruler.\" (Abu Dawood). So, where are the brave hearts? Where are those who will stand and say, \"No, this war is not for us\"?\n\nWake up, O humanity. Wars may be waged by leaders, but they are fought by the poor, the oppressed, and the innocent. Stop marching to the beat of deception. Remember Allah, who says:\n\n\"And do not commit abuse on the earth, spreading corruption.\" (Surah Al-Baqarah 2:60).\n\nWar, unless in self-defense, is corruption. It is a betrayal of the trust Allah has given us to be caretakers of this Earth.\n\nMay Peace, Mercy, and Blessings of Allah be Upon You Also. \ud83c\udf0d", "upvoteCount": 1, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=1477743829281619"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 7116}, "description": "The future can learnt from the past.", "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-161809333-100-ehrmimirjechwuipvzlxhgljiawlkiyj.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 1205}, "knowsAbout": "Military History and Wars ", "name": "Harley Vague", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Harley-Vague-1"}, "dateCreated": "2019-10-13T01:55:05.737398+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "Does major war really benefit humanity by thinning out populations and creating millions of jobs?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/Does-major-war-really-benefit-humanity-by-thinning-out-populations-and-creating-millions-of-jobs"}, "text": "Does major war really benefit humanity by thinning out populations and creating millions of jobs?\n\nEven though most people do not realise the true extent of population losses due to war, ethnic cleansing, genocides and attrocities those losses have not had significant impact.\n\nFirstly, world wars have been so named because they have been fought around the world but not all the world has been involved or participated to a large degree. However devastating and horrendous the losses were, the total casualities are only a small proportion of the world\u2019s population.\n\nSecondly, the rebound after these events usually produces a substantial recovery. That is why we have the baby boomer generation. The world\u2019s population boomed after WWII as people celebrated a new beginning.\n\nWar or in particular, the military does produce employment for many. That is a fact. Hilter turned the economic fortunes of Germany around with military spending.", "upvoteCount": 7, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=173740331"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 1237}, "description": "Instructor at Algonquin College (2012-present)", "hasOccupation": {"@type": "Role", "roleName": "Instructor", "startDate": "2012"}, "image": "", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 860}, "name": "James Eaton", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/James-Eaton-31", "worksFor": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Algonquin College"}}, "dateCreated": "2019-02-07T03:56:46.647387+00:00", "dateModified": "2022-03-07T14:43:45.841791+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How big does a city need to be, population wise, before 24 hour services start to be readily available? i.e. in small towns all businesses will close at night, while in large cities, some businesses will stay open around the clock.", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-big-does-a-city-need-to-be-population-wise-before-24-hour-services-start-to-be-readily-available-i-e-in-small-towns-all-businesses-will-close-at-night-while-in-large-cities-some-businesses-will-stay-open-around"}, "text": "Not very big. What makes the difference in many cases is not the absolute size of the city, but how the industries in the city run.\n\nFor instance, I worked several summers in a town of 17 000 in Nova Scotia that had a major supermarket open 24 hours a day, not to mention convenience stores and coffee shops open 24 hours, because it had a number of industries that operated their plants 24 hours a day (including the factory where I had my summer job on the weekend graveyard shift). A couple of times I went grocery shopping at 3 or 4 am, and I was not alone in the store aisles.\n\nAn hour away was a city of 300 000 that had no 24 hour supermarkets or coffee shops, because none of its industries ran 24 hours a day.", "upvoteCount": 5, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=121426423"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 20345}, "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-18601-100-yenzgpkaatrfbxptohnpiqpkwjikhpxs.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 2229}, "name": "Robert Gauthier", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Robert-Gauthier"}, "dateCreated": "2023-04-20T17:03:07.501007+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How long does it take to rebuild a city/country after a nuclear attack? How many people could live in such an area during reconstruction?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-long-does-it-take-to-rebuild-a-city-country-after-a-nuclear-attack-How-many-people-could-live-in-such-an-area-during-reconstruction"}, "text": "Any country can be divided into two parts. The \"A-country\" is the big cities, the industrial and population centers and the resource concentration they represent. Big cities got to be that way because they are in desirable locations,near good ports, river crossings or mountain passes. When the city goes, so does the locations. The \"B-country\" is everything else. In effect the A-country represents big vulnerable collections of assets gathered into single spots. The B-country represents dispersed ranges of resources spread over large areas.This is a very important distinction. The relative value of the A-country and the B-country depends on the country and society involved. However one thing is constant, the support and supplies that the A-country needs to survive comes from the B-country. Given time, the B-country will rebuild the A-country. The survival of the B-country is, therefore, critical while the survival of the A-country might not be. Now, the primary asset of the B-country is its population; they are the ones who will generate resources from the B-country and turn them into product. So, the critical thing for a post nuclear environment is population. Save as much of that as we can and we're a jump ahead\n\nSo we're in a race. Can we rebuild the B-country so that its firstly self-sustaining without the services provided by the A-country while the stockpile of pre-attack assets survive. Can we reconstruct a working society fast enough so that we can feed enough people to keep going? Can the surviving women bear enough children (and survive doing so) to replace the death toll. For the loss won't stop with the attack. Diseases we consider trivial today, measles, chickenpox, influenza, will be mass killers. No medical treatment. Unless your lucky enough to be where some medical facilities have survived, a broken leg that gets infected is likely to be a death sentence. Its possible to look on this world as a 17th century US colonial environment and there's a lot of truth in that. The downside is that the colonial pioneers didn't have the decaying charnel houses of the cities to worry about. This is another key thing to bear in mind; many more people will die after a nuclear exchange and will die in it.\n\nEventually when conditions permit, our new society moves back to rebuild the A-country. It'll be a long, long time before there is another Federal Government(such things need technology to survive - a calculated guess is that it would take two centuries before a powerful central government evolved again - if it evolves again).\n\nExcerpted from:\n\nNuclear Warfare 101 [ https://www.giantbomb.com/fallout-3/3030-20504/forums/nuclear-warfare-101-wall-of-text-alert-2999/ ]", "upvoteCount": 2, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=1477743660556900"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 33620}, "description": "I help playtest turn-based strategic wargames.", "image": "", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 5527}, "knowsAbout": "World War II", "name": "Doug Hensley", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Doug-Hensley-8"}, "dateCreated": "2019-06-24T03:53:39.696026+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "What do the cities/towns etc. (pictures optional) look like now that were once warzones in WWI/WWII?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/What-do-the-cities-towns-etc-pictures-optional-look-like-now-that-were-once-warzones-in-WWI-WWII"}, "text": "Caen was reduced to rubble by bombing and artillery during the fighting in Normandy. You\u2019d have to look with a keen eye to notice it now.\n\nDresden was even more comprehensively destroyed by fire bombing. This takes only minimal perceptiveness to discern now. There is no old quarter, no historic core such as graces, oh, say, Delft. There is an old cathedral right by the Elbe, but that\u2019s about it. Otherwise, things are, or at any rate in 1991 were, kind of tatty modern utilitarian construction.\n\nAnd the mind\u2019s eye can hardly fail to supply the firestorm, and the river\u2019s contribution to saving just the few stone buildings that graced its banks.\n\nThe battlefield of Verdun used to be home to several villages. They were utterly destroyed and never rebuilt. Mines and unexploded ordinance still infest the land. Trees have grown up but none are mature, and the shape of the ground itself resembles a storm wracked sea. There is no flat place. The mind\u2019s eye works it out. These are somewhat slumped shell craters, one atop the other. Superficially, there\u2019s not much to see. Keep looking, and it\u2019s mute tragedy.", "upvoteCount": 1, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=149560495"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 854}, "description": "lives in Leicester, UK", "homeLocation": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Leicester, UK"}, "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-285561779-100-oiomtgkfevfrvadruodjzkqjsgepfkzf.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 172}, "name": "Guy Raynor-Edwards", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Guy-Raynor-Edwards"}, "dateCreated": "2020-12-26T11:04:21.777099+00:00", "image": [{"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-0b2276ef073918f99d58f8d2f5fa5dc4-lq"}, {"@type": "ImageObject", "contentUrl": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-a17aae15ab5f32a4e62bea0fa0c0c5a9-lq"}], "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How long does it take for a sacked or looted city to recover in history? Can a completely razed city be rebuilt?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-long-does-it-take-for-a-sacked-or-looted-city-to-recover-in-history-Can-a-completely-razed-city-be-rebuilt"}, "text": "Here is a fairly recent example. 10 years apart.\n\n", "upvoteCount": 3, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=256446951"}, {"@type": "Answer", "author": {"@type": "Person", "agentInteractionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/ReplyAction", "userInteractionCount": 2809}, "description": "former Military Intelligence Work (1979-1999)", "hasOccupation": {"@type": "Role", "endDate": "1999", "roleName": "Military Intelligence Work", "startDate": "1979"}, "image": "https://qph.cf2.quoracdn.net/main-thumb-943919373-100-fiagpumhilclubhxxtfbsjjhknrcsgkj.jpeg", "interactionStatistic": {"@type": "InteractionCounter", "interactionType": "http://schema.org/FollowAction", "userInteractionCount": 664}, "name": "Ronald Carpenter", "url": "https://www.quora.com/profile/Ronald-Carpenter-27"}, "dateCreated": "2022-04-29T20:38:58.577079+00:00", "parentItem": {"@type": "Comment", "text": "How would warfare change if everyone was concentrated into giant cities?", "url": "https://www.quora.com/How-would-warfare-change-if-everyone-was-concentrated-into-giant-cities"}, "text": "If everyone was in giant cities, war planning would be greatly simplified, and war materiel much cheaper. Instead of nukes you could find ways to disrupt the city water or power supply, to block food deliveries from the surrounding agricultural region, to harass or kill the resistance \u2026\n\nIf you don\u2019t have to spread your power all across the landscape, war is simplified.", "upvoteCount": 2, "url": "https://www.quora.com/When-war-affects-a-modern-city-how-long-does-it-take-after-fighting-ends-before-city-life-gets-back-to-normal-businesses-operating-at-prewar-levels-population-growth-etc?top_ans=352994415"}], "text": "When war affects a modern city, how long does it take after fighting ends before city life gets back to normal -- businesses operating at prewar levels, population growth, etc.?", "upvoteCount": 5}}
        </script>
